.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt BOOTBRIDGECTL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bootbridgectl
.Nd inspect and steer a running bootbridge
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar command
.Op Ar flags
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
talks to the control socket of a
.Xr bootbridge 8
that is running, on the same machine: it reports the interfaces it
bridges and the peers it knows, prints statistics and the frames that
pass through, and changes what
.Xr bootbridge 8
prints while it runs.
Every command prints one line of JSON the bridge sends;
.Fl json
passes those lines through unchanged, for
.Xr jq 1 .
.Pp
The socket is
.Fl ctl
of
.Xr bootbridge 8 :
.Pa /run/bootbridge/bootbridge.sock
when it runs as root
.Pq or Pa /var/run/bootbridge/bootbridge.sock ,
where there is no Pa /run ,
a per-user socket under
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
or
.Pa /tmp
otherwise.
With several bridges on one machine, give
.Nm
.Fl socket
the path
.Xr bootbridge 8
logged when it started.
.Ev BOOTBRIDGE_CTL_SOCKET
is the default when no
.Fl socket
is given.
Anyone who can write the socket sees every frame bridged and can change
what is printed.
.Pp
The commands are:
.Bl -tag -width "dump [-i name]"
.It Cm status
The bridge's version, uptime, configuration file, interface, MAC, socket
path, and packet printing settings.
.It Cm peers Oo Fl i Ar name Oc
The UDP peers of every transport: the configured ones, the ones learned
from the frames they sent, when each was last heard from, and which
Ethernet addresses were learned from each.
With the LAN side, the hosts seen there with their IP addresses.
.Fl i
limits the output to one side,
.Cm lan
or
.Cm udp ,
or to one transport by name.
.It Cm stats Oo Fl w Oc
Packets, bytes and errors per side, once, or every second with
.Fl w .
.It Cm tail Oo Fl n Ar count Oc
The last
.Ar count
frames the bridge forwarded, 20 by default, 0 for all it remembers.
.It Cm tail Oo Fl t Ar count Oc Fl f
The remembered frames again, then every new one until interrupted;
.Fl t
says how many to replay (50 by default) and
.Fl n
stops after that many.
.It Cm log Oo Fl i Ar name Oc Oo Fl n Ar count Oc Oo Fl t Ar count Oc
Every frame as it passes, like
.Xr bootbridge 8
.Fl print .
.It Cm dump Oo Fl i Ar name Oc Oo Fl n Ar count Oc Oo Fl t Ar count Oc
The same with a hex and ASCII dump of each frame, like
.Xr bootbridge 8
.Fl X .
.It Cm set Oo Fl print Ar on Ns | Ns off Oc Oo Fl dump Ar on Ns | Ns off Oc Oo Fl iface Ar name Oc
Change what
.Xr bootbridge 8
prints to its own terminal, as its single keys do, and print the new
settings.
.El
.Pp
All commands take
.Fl socket Ar path
and
.Fl json .
.Bl -tag -width "-socket path" -offset indent -compact
.It Fl socket Ar path
The control socket to talk to.
.It Fl json
Print the JSON lines the bridge sends.
.El
.Pp
A streaming command stops when the terminal interrupts it, when
.Xr bootbridge 8
stops, or when
.Fl n
frames have been printed.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 1 if the socket cannot be reached or the bridge
reports an error, and 2 for invalid usage.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Ask a bridge started as a service what it knows:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl status
.Dl # bootbridgectl peers
.Pp
Watch a client boot, remembering what passed before you started:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl dump -t 100
.Pp
Follow the frames of one side and count them:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl tail -f -i udp -n 20
.Pp
Turn on the bridge's own packet printing for the LAN side:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl set -print on -iface lan
.Pp
Script it:
.Pp
.Dl # bootbridgectl status -json | jq .uptime
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr jq 1 ,
.Xr bootbridge 8
